WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.620
Star Wars: The Last Jedi featured a Skywalker
leaving his lightsaber behind - but will Star

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:09.800
Wars: Episode IX see another member of the
legendary family pick it up during a pivotal

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:10.300
scene?

00:00:10.340 --> 00:00:14.700
According to leaked information regarding
the forthcoming installment in the space-faring

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.900
saga, it's apparently a guarantee.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:21.620
Express recently obtained a description of
a "tantalizing" Episode IX scene that features

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.780
Carrie Fisher's General Leia wielding a "classic
Jedi lightsaber" - the Skywalker saber.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.180
Not only is Leia said to hold it in the film,
but the headstrong Rebel Alliance leader will

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.080
reportedly also ignite it.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:37.620
The leak claims that, during an important
sequence in Episode IX, Leia is on a world

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:43.350
"that appears to be a mix between Endor, Tatooine,
and Jakku - so basically, the middle of nowhere.

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:44.100
"Where are you from?

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:45.220
Nowhere.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:47.240
No one's from nowhere.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:47.740
Jakku.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:50.120
Alright, that is pretty much nowhere."

00:00:50.540 --> 00:00:54.720
Speculation is that this could be the planet
of Batuu, located in the Outer Rim.

00:00:54.860 --> 00:00:59.120
Wearing a black outfit that looks "almost
like a robe," Leia is seen standing next to

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.799
Maz Kanata, C-3PO, and R2-D2, "holding the
Skywalker lightsaber and examining it."

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:06.700
The scene description reads, quote:

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.480
"She begins to hold it in a Jedi-like stance
and activates it, pointing it towards a city

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.280
that looks like it has gone through battle."

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:18.400
Though anyone, Force-sensitive or not, can
ignite a lightsaber, it's interesting to imagine

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:20.900
Leia activating one given her ties to the
Force.

00:01:21.229 --> 00:01:25.740
Film fans were first made aware of Leia's
Force sensitivity over 30 years ago, in The

00:01:25.751 --> 00:01:30.420
Empire Strikes Back, during the scene in which
her twin brother Luke implemented the Force

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.700
to reach out to her - thus granting Leia the
opportunity to save Luke from falling to his

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.240
death on Cloud City.

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:41.579
Her Force responsiveness was again explored
- this time in a much bigger way - in 2017's

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:42.380
The Last Jedi.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.600
During an intense battle with the First Order,
Leia and a number of her fellow Rebels were

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:50.720
flung into deep space after an explosion destroyed
part of Leia's ship.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:52.760
It looked as though Leia was done for.

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:56.929
But just as viewers began preparing to say
goodbye to Leia, the General channeled the

00:01:56.929 --> 00:01:59.680
Force and used its power to pull herself back
onto the ship.

00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:04.289
However, just because Leia will purportedly
get a lightsaber in Episode IX doesn't mean

00:02:04.289 --> 00:02:07.840
that she's channeling the Force - and it also
doesn't mean that she has risen to replace

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.500
her brother Luke as the surviving Jedi of
the Skywalker family.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.099
The leak concludes with an explicit statement
alleging the exact opposite:

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.720
"This does not indicate that Leia is a Jedi.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.810
The stance is done more in a victorious way.

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.160
It could be the site of a First Order defeat."

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:27.100
Overall, Episode IX including a scene in which
Leia activates a lightsaber seems entirely

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:31.239
feasible - and it wouldn't be difficult for
Abrams and the horde of hyper-talented special

00:02:31.240 --> 00:02:36.080
effects artists on board the project to create
a Leia-led sequence several years after Fisher's

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:36.580
death.

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.140
The Episode IX creative team will make use
of rotoscoping techniques to take previously

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:45.540
captured footage of Fisher as Leia in The
Force Awakens and The Last Jedi and place

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:50.650
it over newly shot material, creating brand-new
scenes for Episode IX without resorting to

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.620
making a CGI version of Leia.

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:54.180
We all know how that looks.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:57.440
But, at the end of the day, this is an unsubstantiated
leak.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.260
As with every rumor, fan-made theory, and
wild piece of speculations, you should take

00:03:02.260 --> 00:03:04.120
these details with a grain of salt.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:07.900
Sure, the information sounds awesome, but
none of it has been confirmed.

00:03:07.909 --> 00:03:12.930
Lucasfilm and the entire Episode IX cast and
crew are keeping an air-tight lid on details,

00:03:12.930 --> 00:03:17.159
so we can't imagine that anyone involved with
the upcoming pic will be rushing to comment

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:18.740
on this new leak anytime soon.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.780
Everyone will just have to wait until Star
Wars: Episode IX opens in December 2019 to

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.520
find out if Leia truly does light up a saber.

